course: dictionary
lesson: 5659
title: Literally: exactly what it says
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Literally» means exactly as the words say, with nothing added. When you take something literally, you believe only the plain meaning. «It's raining cats and dogs» is not literally true, because no animals are falling from the sky. Its real meaning is that it is raining very hard.
explain: People often say literally just to make something sound stronger, even when it is not true. If someone says «I literally died of boredom», they are exaggerating. Ask yourself: are these words really true? If not, the speaker is using them for effect.
explain: Literally can also mean word for word. A literal translation follows each word of the original. «xiàwǔ» means afternoon, literally «after noon», so the parts of the word show you how it is built.
fact: Literally means exactly as the words say, not as a figure of speech.
fact: «It's raining cats and dogs» is not literally true; it means it is raining very hard.
fact: Many people say literally just to sound stronger, even when it is not true.
fact: «xiàwǔ» means afternoon, literally «after noon»; «shàngwǔ» means late morning, literally «before noon».
fact: «lǎnchóng» means lazybones, literally «lazy worm», and parents use it fondly on Sunday mornings.
exercise: Maya says, «I literally died of boredom!» Is she really dead? | Yes, she is really dead | *No, she is exaggerating to sound stronger | She is telling a true story about a hospital
exercise: What does «xiàwǔ» literally mean? | before noon | *after noon | lazy worm
exercise: Which one means exactly what the words say? | *A translation that follows each word | She is over the moon | It's raining cats and dogs
test: Tom says, «I literally have a million jobs to do.» Does he really have a million? | Yes, he counted them all | *No, he is exaggerating | He is talking about money
test: In «lǎnchóng» means lazybones, literally «lazy worm», what does literally tell us? | A joke about a funny animal | A word that is only used for worms | *The meaning of each part of the word, word for word
exam: What does «take it literally» mean? | Forget it straight away | Do it with a lot of energy | *Believe only the plain meaning of the words
